/* THE CARD THAT PAINTED ITSELF OFF THE SHEET.
   This was five inline styles on the component. One of them was
   `background: rgba(245, 166, 35, 0.06)` — a SHORTHAND, which does not tint the
   card, it replaces the card's fill with a 6% amber and lets whatever is behind
   the panel show through. Behind a panel is the app canvas, which in the light
   theme is a dark slab that every other card covers with its own opaque white.
   So on paper the whole card came out as rgb(43,47,50) with the sheet's near
   black ink on it: heading 1.29:1, body 1.19:1, eyebrow 1.96:1, both buttons
   under 2:1. Not a contrast nit — the card was unreadable.

   background-image, not background: the tint now rides on top of whatever fill
   the card already resolves to, so it is a tint in both themes and there is no
   second copy of the panel colour to keep in step. And it sits AFTER
   .workspace-section-card rather than before, because that rule's shorthand
   resets background-image to none and would otherwise erase this one.

   :root[data-theme] is not decoration. `:root[data-theme='light'] .panel` sets
   `background: #ffffff` — a shorthand at (0,3,0), which beat a bare
   .blog-reminder-card at (0,1,0) and reset background-image to none no matter
   where in the file it sat. Measured that way first: the tint simply was not
   there. This selector matches that weight exactly and wins on source order,
   which is the only reason it is written this way. */
:root[data-theme] .blog-reminder-card {
  border-color: var(--cedge-amber-6);
  background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(245, 166, 35, 0.06), rgba(245, 166, 35, 0.06));
}

